---
// English mirror of works/sumo-tournament.astro.
import CaseStudy from "../../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const sections = [
  { id: "en-bref", label: "In short" },
  { id: "le-sujet", label: "The subject" },
  { id: "ce-que-jai-fait", label: "What I did" },
  { id: "difficile", label: "What was hard" },
  { id: "retiens", label: "What I take away" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Sumo Tournament"
  kicker="Event, design and code"
  didascalie="Event website for a fictional sumo tournament. Paris."
  sections={sections}
  lang="en"
>
  <h2 id="en-bref">In short</h2>
  <p>
    Designing and developing a complete website for an imaginary sumo
    tournament in Paris. Not just a showcase: a real platform with ticketing
    and a back office to manage tournament data, wrestlers, bouts and bookings.
    The project where design truly meets code.
  </p>
  <p class="text-voile">
    Training project. Design and production end to end. One week, in an
    intense sprint.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/images/sumo-home.png" alt="Sumo Tournament website home page" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>The tournament home page. A contemporary interface for a highly codified universe.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="le-sujet">The subject</h2>
  <p>
    Sumo is a world of rare visual richness. Centuries of codes, rituals and
    symbols. The challenge was to translate this strong, highly codified
    culture into a contemporary web interface, without betraying it and
    without falling into cliché. Finding the point where respect for the
    universe meets the clarity of a modern interface.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ce-que-jai-fait">What I did</h2>

  <h3>The design</h3>
  <p>
    Sitemap, wireframes for the main pages, component design. This is the core
    of my work and where I was fully in charge.
  </p>

  <h3>The production</h3>
  <p>
    To bring the project to life, I handled the front-end integration (HTML,
    CSS) and the dynamic side (PHP, MySQL database), using AI as a code
    copilot. My role wasn't to write every line as an expert, but to steer the
    build: understanding the architecture, asking the right questions,
    integrating, testing, debugging until the site actually worked.
  </p>

  <div class="my-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <img src="/images/sumo-header.png" alt="Sumo Tournament site header" loading="lazy" class="w-full rounded-[2px] border border-voile/15" />
    <img src="/images/sumo-event.png" alt="Tournament event page" loading="lazy" class="w-full rounded-[2px] border border-voile/15" />
    <img src="/images/sumo-history.png" alt="History of sumo page" loading="lazy" class="w-full rounded-[2px] border border-voile/15" />
    <img src="/images/sumo-contact.png" alt="Site contact page" loading="lazy" class="w-full rounded-[2px] border border-voile/15" />
    <img src="/images/sumo-test-card.png" alt="Event card (test card)" loading="lazy" class="w-full rounded-[2px] border border-voile/15" />
  </div>

  <h2 id="difficile">What was hard</h2>
  <p>
    Making the site actually work. Understanding why a page didn't render as
    expected, why a piece of data didn't come through, and fixing things until
    everything responded. It's a different kind of rigor from design, more
    binary. Either it works or it doesn't, and you have to understand why.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retiens">What I take away</h2>
  <p>
    The moment the site came to life, when the pages became dynamic and my
    mockups went from image to a working tool that responds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Above all, this project taught me to produce with AI without letting it
    take over. Knowing what I want, phrasing it clearly, checking what comes
    back, correcting. It's a project-lead skill as much as a designer's:
    getting the right result by orchestrating the right tools, without needing
    to master everything yourself.
  </p>

  <p class="citation">
    Designing means knowing what you want to achieve. Even when other tools do
    the executing.
  </p>

  <div class="meta">
    <p><strong>Tools</strong>: Figma, VS Code, HTML, CSS, PHP, MySQL.</p>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
